course: religion-ethics
lesson: 2
title: Adam and Eve, Cain and Abel
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Genesis 2 and 3 God places Adam and Eve, the first man and woman, in the Garden of Eden and lets them eat from every tree except one: the tree of the knowledge of good and evil.
explain: A clever serpent persuades Eve that God is keeping something good from her; she eats the fruit and gives some to Adam, and then they hide from God, Adam blames Eve and Eve blames the serpent.
explain: Christians call this first disobedience the Fall, the moment sin and trouble came into the world, yet God still makes clothes for Adam and Eve before sending them out of the garden.
explain: Their son Cain grows jealous of his brother Abel and kills him; when God asks where Abel is, Cain answers "Am I my brother's keeper?", and the Bible's answer is yes.
explain: Even so, God puts a mark on Cain so no one kills him in revenge, because justice is not the same as revenge.
explain: For a child today: temptation often starts with doubting that a rule is for our good, owning up is braver than hiding and blaming, and jealousy must be stopped early before it grows.
fact: Adam and Eve are the first man and woman in the Bible, made by God and placed in the Garden of Eden.
fact: Christians believe people were made to be friends with God and with each other.
fact: God told Adam and Eve they could eat from every tree except the tree of the knowledge of good and evil.
fact: The Bible does not say the fruit was an apple; that idea came much later from paintings and stories.
fact: The serpent persuaded Eve that God was keeping something good from her; she ate the fruit and gave some to Adam.
fact: After eating, Adam and Eve hid from God; Adam blamed Eve, and Eve blamed the serpent.
fact: Christians call the first disobedience the Fall: the moment when sin and trouble entered the world.
fact: Christians believe everyone does wrong sometimes and needs God's help to put things right.
fact: Even after sending Adam and Eve out of the garden, God made clothes for them.
fact: Christians differ on whether Adam and Eve were two real people or a story about all humanity (disputed).
fact: Cain and Abel were Adam and Eve's sons; Cain grew jealous of Abel and killed him.
fact: When God asked where Abel was, Cain answered "Am I my brother's keeper?"
fact: God put a mark on Cain so that no one would kill him in revenge.
exercise: Who were Adam and Eve? | The first man and woman, placed in the Garden of Eden | The first king and queen of Egypt | Noah's son and daughter on the ark | Two angels who guarded the garden gate with a sword of fire
exercise: What were people made for, according to the story? | To be friends with God and with each other | To work as servants who never get to rest or play | To live alone, each in their own separate garden | To fight the wild animals for the land and food
exercise: Which tree were Adam and Eve told not to eat from? | The tree of the knowledge of good and evil | The olive tree that grew in the middle of the field | Every fruit tree in the whole garden | The apple tree by the river gate
exercise: How can a rule protect us even when we do not fully understand it? | The one who made it may see dangers we cannot see yet | Rules only protect us if we understand every part of them fully | Rules are there only to spoil our fun | A rule protects us only if we chose it ourselves
exercise: How did the serpent trick Eve? | It made her doubt that God's rule was for her good | It forced the fruit into her mouth | It told her that Adam had already eaten | It promised her a new garden of her own
exercise: What does the serpent's trick teach about temptation? | It often starts by making us doubt that rules are for our good | It always starts with someone hitting us | It only happens to grown-ups, never to children or young people | It is easy to spot because it always looks ugly
exercise: What did Adam and Eve do after eating the fruit? | They hid, and then blamed someone else | They ran to God at once and said sorry | They ate from every other tree as well | They went back to work as if nothing happened
exercise: Why is owning up braver than hiding and blaming? | It means facing the truth and taking responsibility | Because hiding is always against the law | Because blaming others never works anyway | Because owning up means you will never be punished for anything
exercise: What do Christians call the first disobedience of Adam and Eve? | The Fall | The Flood | The Exodus | The Sabbath
exercise: What do Christians believe about people because of the Fall? | Everyone does wrong sometimes and needs God's help to put things right | Only bad people ever do wrong things | Children can never do anything wrong | People can always fix every wrong on their own
exercise: What did God do for Adam and Eve even after sending them out? | He made clothes for them | He let them come back the next day | He sent them to live with Noah | He took away all their work
exercise: What does God's care after the Fall show? | God's care continues even when people fail | God stops caring for people who make mistakes | God only cares for people who never fail | God was not really angry at all
exercise: Why did Cain kill his brother Abel? | He grew jealous of Abel | Abel had stolen Cain's sheep | Abel had hit Cain first | God had told Cain to do it
exercise: What does the story of Cain and Abel teach about jealousy? | Left unchecked it can grow into terrible harm, so master anger early | Jealousy is harmless as long as you keep it a secret from everyone around you | Jealousy only matters between grown-ups | Jealousy goes away by itself if you ignore it
exercise: What did Cain say when God asked where Abel was? | "Am I my brother's keeper?" | "Here I am, Lord." | "I am sorry for what I did." | "Abel has gone to the city."
exercise: What is the Bible's answer to "Am I my brother's keeper?" | Yes, we are responsible for one another | No, everyone looks after only themselves | Only parents are their children's keepers | Only priests must look after other people
exercise: Why did God put a mark on Cain? | So that no one would kill him in revenge | So that everyone would laugh at him | So that he could never work again | So that he would forget what he had done
exercise: What does the mark of Cain teach about justice and revenge? | Justice is not the same as revenge; even a wrongdoer is protected from endless revenge | Revenge is the best kind of justice | A wrongdoer deserves no protection at all | Justice means doing back exactly what was done
test: my mate says Eve ate an apple, it's in the Bible. is it? | No, the Bible just says fruit; the apple came later from paintings | Yes, it clearly says it was a big red apple from a tree by the river | No, it was a banana in the Bible | Yes, and Adam ate a pear
test: "It wasn't me, it was HIM!" your brother shouts when the vase breaks. Which Bible scene is he copying? | Adam blaming Eve and Eve blaming the serpent | Noah sending out the dove to look for dry land | Joseph forgiving his brothers in Egypt at last | God resting on the seventh day after making all
test: Sam, whose family has no religion, asks: "So do Christians think kids are born evil?" What is the fairest answer? | No, they believe people are made good but everyone does wrong sometimes and needs God's help | Yes, Christians think children are wicked monsters | Christians think nobody ever does wrong | Christians think only grown-ups can sin
test: shut up, I'm not my sister's babysitter, she can sort herself out. Which story answers this? | Cain and Abel: "Am I my brother's keeper?" and the answer yes | Adam and Eve hiding among the trees so that God would not find them | The serpent and the forbidden tree | God making clothes after the Fall
test: Your friend says: "That kid broke my pencil, so I'm gonna wreck his whole bag." What does the mark of Cain teach? | Justice is not endless revenge; paying back bigger makes it worse | Revenge is fair if you do it bigger | The Bible says always pay back twice as much as was done to you, at least | You must never tell anyone about it
test: Lily's mum told her not to touch the cooker. Her friend says, "Your mum just doesn't want you to have fun." What is the friend doing, like the serpent? | Making her doubt that the rule is for her good | Teaching her how to use the cooker safely herself | Helping her to obey her mum and keep out of danger | Telling her the plain truth about how cookers work
test: Oliver keeps scowling at his brother who got picked for the team. "I hate him. Bet he cheated." What is the lesson from Cain? | Deal with jealousy early, before it grows into harm | Jealousy like that proves the brother must have cheated | Feelings like that never cause any harm | He should stop talking to his brother for good
test: After the Fall, God still made clothes for Adam and Eve. A teacher asks what this tells us. | God keeps caring for people even when they fail | God wanted them to look smart for the long trip ahead | God had forgotten they had done wrong | God was making them into servants
test: my cousin reckons Christians all think Adam and Eve were deffo two real people. true? | Not all; some think so, others read it as a story about all humanity | Yes, every Christian thinks exactly that | No, no Christian ever thought they were real | Christians think they were angels, not people
test: Olympiad judge: "Was the tree rule unfair because Adam and Eve did not understand it?" What is the best answer? | Not necessarily: a good rule can protect us before we understand it | Yes, any rule we do not fully understand is unfair, so it can be ignored | Yes, so they were right to break it | No, because rules never need any reason
test: Judge's follow-up: "Cain broke the worst rule. Why not let anyone take revenge on him?" | Because revenge would only start a chain of more killing | Because Cain had done nothing wrong | Because God did not see what happened | Because nobody else lived near Cain
exam: Identify the principal manner in which, according to Genesis, the serpent induced the first woman to eat of the forbidden tree. | By causing her to doubt the beneficence of the divine command | By compelling her through physical force | By informing her that the tree was not in fact forbidden to her at all | By offering her dominion over the entire garden
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established Christian teaching concerning the Fall. | Sin and its consequences entered the world through the first disobedience | The Fall denotes the deluge which covered the earth | The Fall pertains exclusively to the wicked among humanity | The Fall was succeeded by the total and permanent withdrawal of divine care
exam: State the significance of the interrogative "Am I my brother's keeper?" as understood in the Biblical narrative. | Persons bear responsibility for the welfare of one another | Persons bear responsibility exclusively for themselves and their own affairs | Responsibility for others rests with the clergy alone, and not with the laity | The question was uttered by Abel concerning his brother Cain
exam: Ascertain the purpose for which, according to Genesis, a mark was placed upon Cain. | To afford him protection from acts of vengeance | To render him an object of public ridicule | To signify his appointment as a ruler | To indicate that he had been pardoned of all consequences
